          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "authMiddleware treats every path matching the unanchored regex /v1/datasets/.+ (and /v1/runs/.+/public, /v1/runs/.+/feedback, /auth/.+) as a \"public route\" and skips ALL authentication. On line 82 it unconditionally sets ctx.state.projectId from the attacker-controlled query string (ctx.request?.query?.projectId). The tenant-ownership control \u2014 `select id from project where id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and org_id = ${ctx.state.orgId}` on line 120, which throws \"Project not found\" \u2014 exists ONLY inside the authenticated branch (after verifyJwt on line 112). On the public-route branch (lines 90-105) there is no equivalent check: when isPublicRoute && !bearerToken, control falls straight to `await next()` on line 94 with projectId still equal to the raw query parameter. Therefore an unauthenticated request such as GET /v1/datasets/?projectId=<victim-project-uuid> with no Authorization header is served with the victim's projectId in ctx.state, and downstream handlers (e.g. datasets/index.ts:13 `select * from dataset d where project_id = ${projectId}`) trust it. Missing authorization/tenant-binding control on the public-route path.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. authMiddleware is installed globally (index.ts:34) ahead of all routes. It sets ctx.state.projectId from the raw query string unconditionally at utils.ts:82, then at utils.ts:84-86 tests ctx.path against an unanchored RegExp(\"/v1/datasets/.+\") (line 76). With no Authorization header, bearerToken is undefined and validateUUID(undefined)===false (utils/misc.ts:20-24), so the branch at line 90 falls through to `await next()` at line 94 with projectId still equal to the attacker-supplied value. The sole tenant-ownership control \u2014 `select id from project where id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and org_id = ${ctx.state.orgId}` throwing \"Project not found\" (lines 120-126) \u2014 exists only after verifyJwt (line 112); there is no equivalent on the public-route path (org_id appears exactly once in the file). Downstream handlers trust ctx.state.projectId as the only tenant predicate (datasets/index.ts:22-44 -> datasets/utils.ts:6 and :44). CORRECTION to the analyst's PoC: `/v1/datasets/` does NOT match `.+` (ctx.path has no trailing char), so the GET \"/\" route still requires a JWT; the actually-exploitable request is `GET /v1/datasets/<uuid-or-slug>?projectId=<victim-project-uuid>` with no auth header. Impact is broader than claimed: the same regex also exposes DELETE /v1/datasets/:id (datasets/index.ts:111-117), which has NO project_id predicate at all, plus PATCH /:id, POST /prompts and DELETE /prompts/:id \u2014 unauthenticated cross-tenant read AND destructive write. The graph labels the dataset handler calls reachable_from_entry=false/hop_distance=-1, but that is the documented arrow-function/route-callback under-linking: the middleware calls at lines 82/84/90/94/120 are all reachable_from_entry=true and the mount chain app.use(v1.routes()) -> v1.use(datasets.routes()) with prefixes \"/v1\" + \"/datasets\" is explicit in source, so I did not downgrade. Exploit-reference step not applicable (first-party logic, no pinned component version) and the corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"."
          },
