        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "datasets.delete(\"/:id\") performs `delete from dataset where id = ${id}` with NO `and project_id = ${projectId}` predicate and no ownership pre-check \u2014 unlike the sibling datasets.patch(\"/:id\") on line 96 which DOES scope with `where id = ${id} and project_id = ${projectId}`. The handler does not even read projectId from ctx.state. Because /v1/datasets/<uuid> matches the publicRoutes regex `/v1/datasets/.+` in auth/utils.ts:62, this destructive route is reachable with no Authorization header at all, so any party who learns (or brute-forces) a dataset UUID can delete another tenant's dataset. Missing authorization control on a destructive route.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. packages/backend/src/api/v1/datasets/index.ts:111-117 registers DELETE /:id whose handler destructures only `const { id } = ctx.params` (never reads ctx.state) and executes sql`delete from dataset where id = ${id}` with NO `and project_id = ${projectId}` predicate and no ownership pre-check. The sibling datasets.patch(\"/:id\") at :96-109 does read projectId from ctx.state and does scope with `where id = ${id} and project_id = ${projectId}` (:105), proving the omission is an inconsistency rather than an intentional design. The unauthenticated-reach element also holds: auth/utils.ts:76 lists `new RegExp('/v1/datasets/.+')` in publicRoutes, tested against ctx.path at :85; at :90-96 the branch `isPublicRoute && (!bearerToken || validateUUID(bearerToken))` calls bare `await next()` with no verifyJwt and no 401, so DELETE /v1/datasets/<uuid> executes with no Authorization header. Route is genuinely live, traced through source wiring (not graph attribution): index.ts:34 app.use(authMiddleware) is the sole auth layer, index.ts:41 app.use(v1.routes()) -> v1/index.ts:19,39 (prefix \"/v1\", v1.use(datasets.routes())) -> datasets/index.ts:10 (prefix \"/datasets\"). No compensating control exists: utils/db.ts is a plain postgres() client with no RLS or per-tenant session variable, and utils/ratelimit.ts only throttles (150 req/min/IP, max:150) without rejecting unauthenticated requests. I discount the lead's reachable_from_entry=false concern because that flag is uniformly false for every node in this file, including the route registrations and the correctly-scoped patch sibling, i.e. the documented arrow-function under-linking artifact. Two corrections to the analyst: the datasets publicRoutes regex is at auth/utils.ts:76, not :62 (:62 is only the array declaration), and the \"brute-forces a dataset UUID\" framing is weak since UUIDv4 entropy plus the rate limiter makes enumeration infeasible -- the realistic exploit path is a party who legitimately learns an id (cross-tenant authenticated user, ex-project-member, or an id leaked via URL/logs/shared links), which still constitutes missing authorization enabling permanent cross-tenant data destruction. Exploit-reference grounding was unavailable (corpus reported \"not indexed\"), which does not affect the verdict as this is a first-party authorization logic flaw rather than a versioned-dependency CVE."
          },
