        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-640",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The /reset-password handler never validates the submitted token against the stored account.recovery_token column, and never invalidates it after use. /request-password-reset writes `update account set recovery_token = ${token} where id = ${user.id}` (auth/index.ts:180), but a graph-wide search shows recovery_token is NEVER read in any query in the repo \u2014 the column is write-only, so the intended single-use binding control is not enforced. /reset-password only calls verifyJwt<{email}>(token) (signature+exp) and then does `update account set password_hash = ${passwordHash} where email = ${email}`. Two consequences: (a) a reset token is infinitely replayable for its full lifetime; (b) because signJwt payloads carry no purpose/type/audience claim, ANY {email}-shaped JWT signed with JWT_SECRET is accepted as a password-reset token \u2014 including the email-confirmation token minted by `signJwt({ email })` at utils/emails.ts:14, which uses signJwt's default ONE_MONTH expiry (auth/utils.ts:32-33) rather than the ONE_HOUR used for resets. Missing token-binding / token-purpose-separation control.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed on all points. (a) The /reset-password handler (packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/index.ts:194-218) validates only the JWT signature+exp via verifyJwt<{email}>(token) at line 203, then updates password_hash keyed solely on the payload email (207-209). It never compares the submitted token to account.recovery_token and never clears it, so a reset token is replayable for its entire 1-hour lifetime and stale tokens remain valid after reissue. (b) A repo-wide grep (independent of the graph) shows recovery_token appears exactly twice in the whole tree: the column declaration at packages/db/init.sql:31 and the lone write at auth/index.ts:180 \u2014 zero reads, confirming the single-use binding control is write-only and unenforced. (c) The more severe consequence is token-purpose confusion: signJwt (auth/utils.ts:33-46) emits only exp/iat/nbf with no aud/typ/purpose claim, and verifyJwt (48-53) supplies no audience or claim constraints, so ANY {email}-shaped JWT signed with JWT_SECRET is accepted as a reset token. utils/emails.ts:14 mints exactly that shape with signJwt({ email }) using the DEFAULT ONE_MONTH expiry (auth/utils.ts:32,35) and delivers it in a URL query string (emails.ts:16), where it persists in email bodies, browser history, referrer headers and server logs \u2014 making a 30-day email-confirmation link redeemable for password reset and full account takeover. (d) On exploitability I did NOT accept the graph's reachable_from_entry=false: every node in the file reports hop_distance=-1 including the top-level auth.post(\"/reset-password\") registration itself, which is a clear arrow-function under-linking artifact since route registration executes at import time. Real source confirms reach \u2014 app.use(auth.routes()) at packages/backend/src/index.ts:42 mounts the router, and the publicRoutes entry new RegExp(\"/auth/.+\") at auth/utils.ts:63 makes /auth/reset-password unauthenticated, with authMiddleware short-circuiting before any bearer-token check. Pre-auth, network-reachable. Corrected the analyst's claimed range from 194-215 to the actual 194-218. exploit_search not applicable: first-party logic flaw with no versioned component in the vulnerable path."
          },
