        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "projects.delete(\"/:projectId\") takes projectId from ctx.params and deletes it with `delete from project where id = ${projectId}` \u2014 with NO predicate binding that project to the caller's org. The only check performed is a COUNT of the caller's own projects (`select count(*)::int from project where org_id = ${orgId}`, used solely to enforce \"an organization must have at least one project\"); that count never constrains WHICH project is deleted. Any authenticated user whose own org holds more than one project can therefore delete an arbitrary project belonging to any other organization by supplying its UUID. Missing resource-ownership authorization check.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. projects/index.ts:70-89 reads projectId from ctx.params and executes `delete from project where id = ${projectId}` (line 78) with no predicate tying the row to the caller's org. The only identity-bearing query is `select count(*)::int from project where org_id = ${orgId}` (line 75), which counts the ATTACKER's own projects and merely gates whether a delete occurs (\"an organization must have at least one project\") \u2014 it never constrains WHICH project is deleted. I specifically checked the one thing that could refute the lead: the upstream ownership check in authMiddleware. It does NOT cover this route. auth/utils.ts:82 sets ctx.state.projectId from `ctx.request?.query?.projectId` (the QUERY STRING), and the ownership query at auth/utils.ts:117-127 (`select id from project where id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and org_id = ${ctx.state.orgId}`) only runs when that value is truthy. A request `DELETE /v1/projects/<victim-uuid>` with no `?projectId=` query param skips the check entirely; ctx.params is not even parsed at middleware time. Supplying `?projectId=<own-project>` likewise passes the check while the delete still targets the path param. The path matches none of the publicRoutes entries (auth/utils.ts:62-78), so a valid JWT is required but orgId is the attacker's own \u2014 i.e. ANY authenticated user whose org holds >1 project can delete an arbitrary project in any other org. The route is live and wired: src/index.ts:34,41 -> v1/index.ts:37 -> prefix \"/projects\", single handler with no route-level guard, and the frontend calls it (frontend/utils/dataHooks.ts:185-188, fetcher.delete on `/projects/${projectId}`). The graph's reachable_from_entry=false is a systemic arrow-function under-linking artifact, not dead code: 114 of 118 backend route registrations are false. The codebase's own convention elsewhere scopes such DML (templates.ts:79, checklists.ts:77-79, radars.ts:283-285 all include the owning predicate), making projects/index.ts:78 the outlier. Severity aggravator I found independently: projects/index.ts:53-57 sets the public api_key equal to project.id, so a victim project's UUID IS its public API key, routinely embedded in client-side SDK code \u2014 target UUIDs are discoverable, not unguessable. No exploit_search applies: first-party app logic, no versioned third-party component to pin. The analyst's location and CWE were accurate."
          },
          "locations": [
