        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "projects.patch(\"/:projectId\") takes projectId from ctx.params and runs `update project set name = ${name} where id = ${projectId}` with no org/tenant predicate whatsoever \u2014 the handler never even destructures orgId or projectId from ctx.state, so no ownership information is consulted. Any authenticated user can rename any project in any organization by UUID. Missing resource-ownership authorization check.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. The PATCH /v1/projects/:projectId handler takes projectId from the attacker-controlled path param and issues `update project set name = ${name} where id = ${projectId}` with no org_id/tenant predicate and no ctx.state read (projects/index.ts:91-106). Critically, I verified the global middleware does NOT compensate: authMiddleware sets ctx.state.projectId exclusively from the QUERY STRING (auth/utils.ts:82, `ctx.request?.query?.projectId`), and its project-ownership check is gated behind `if (ctx.state.projectId)` (auth/utils.ts:117-127). The frontend's patch helper sends no ?projectId= query param (fetcher.ts:69-78), so on this request ctx.state.projectId is undefined and the ownership check is skipped entirely. The route is not in publicRoutes (auth/utils.ts:62-78), so a valid JWT is required, but any authenticated user's JWT from any org passes. The guard is unfixable from the query side anyway, since the UPDATE keys off ctx.params regardless. Sibling routes show the intended pattern (templates.ts:71,79,95 use `where project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and id = ${ctx.params.id}`), proving the omission is a defect, not design. Reachability is real despite the graph: the router is mounted (v1/index.ts:37 -> src/index.ts:41) and the frontend actively calls it for project rename (dataHooks.ts:181-184,190-196). The graph's reachable_from_entry=false is a false negative \u2014 it reports false for all four routes in the file including projects.get(\"/\"), the endpoint that populates the dashboard, the known arrow-function under-linking artifact. Exploitability is further raised because project UUIDs are not secret: the public API key is set equal to project.id (projects/index.ts:53-57) and returned as public_api_key (line 20), so target IDs leak into client-side SDK configs. Severity HIGH rather than CRITICAL: impact is cross-tenant integrity (rename) only, with no data disclosure. Advisory exploit_search was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\") and no versioned :Dependency is implicated \u2014 this is a first-party authz logic flaw, so the version/CVE comparison workflow does not apply."
          },
