            "text": "runs.get(\"/:id/related\") executes a RECURSIVE CTE seeded with `WHERE r1.id = ${id}` from ctx.params and returns rr.input, rr.output, rr.params, rr.feedback and rr.project_id for the whole run tree, with NO project_id or org_id predicate \u2014 the handler never reads ctx.state at all. Both sibling routes DO enforce scoping: runs.get(\"/:id\") at line 189 scopes by orgId (with an explicit comment about teammates), and runs.get(\"/:id/feedback\") at line 261 scopes by `project_id = ${projectId} and id = ${id}`. Any authenticated user can read another tenant's full LLM prompt/completion payloads by run UUID. Missing authorization check.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. runs.get(\"/:id/related\") (packages/backend/src/api/v1/runs/index.ts:239-259) takes a run UUID from ctx.params.id and runs a RECURSIVE CTE seeded only by `WHERE r1.id = ${id}` (line 246), projecting rr.input, rr.output, rr.params, rr.feedback and rr.project_id (lines 253-254) for the run and its whole descendant tree. The handler never reads ctx.state \u2014 no project_id or org_id predicate at all. Both sibling routes enforce tenant scoping, proving the intended control: :189-193 scopes by `r.project_id in (select id from project where org_id = ${orgId})` with the explicit comment \"Use orgId in case teammates shares URL to run...\" (line 192), and :261-271 scopes by `project_id = ${projectId} and id = ${id}`; :163-179 (/:id/public) additionally requires `is_public = true`, confirming non-public run payloads are meant to be protected. The route is genuinely reachable: src/index.ts:41 app.use(v1.routes()) -> api/v1/index.ts:38 v1.use(runs.routes()) -> router prefix \"/runs\" (runs/index.ts:11) = GET /v1/runs/:id/related. It is NOT in the publicRoutes allowlist (auth/utils.ts:62-78 lists /v1/runs/.+/public, /v1/runs/.+/feedback and /v1/runs/ingest, but not /related), so authMiddleware:108-136 requires a valid JWT \u2014 making this exploitable by ANY authenticated user against ANY other tenant's runs. authMiddleware:117-127 does validate a projectId query param against the caller's org, but the /related handler ignores projectId entirely so that check never constrains this query, and utils/db.ts:5-18 is a plain postgres.js client with no RLS or tenant session variable, so there is no compensating global scoping. Two corrections to the analyst: (a) ${id} is a postgres.js parameterized bind, so this is NOT SQL injection \u2014 it is purely a missing authorization check, and CWE-639 is correct; (b) the graph's reachable_from_entry=false must be disregarded \u2014 I queried all eight route registrations in this file and every one is false, including GET /v1/runs/ (the primary list endpoint), confirming a file-wide koa-router arrow-function under-linking artifact rather than true unreachability. The exploit-reference check was unavailable (corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\") and no meaningful upstream version could be pinned (root package.json:3 is a 1.0.0 monorepo placeholder), so this verdict rests solely on code evidence, with no claim of patched or CVE-matched status. Severity HIGH rather than CRITICAL because exploitation requires knowing a target run UUID (v4, not practically brute-forceable), though UUIDs leak readily via shared URLs, logs and SDK responses, and the recursive CTE amplifies one known UUID into the run's entire descendant tree."
