          "ruleId": "CWE-942",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The CORS policy reflects the caller's Origin verbatim while also enabling credentials, with no allowlist check anywhere. utils/cors.ts:7-9 sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: ctx.get(\"Origin\") || \"*\"` together with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, and the @koa/cors options at line 17 do the same via `origin(ctx) { return ctx.get(\"Origin\") || \"*\" }` with `credentials: true`. There is no comparison of the Origin header against NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL or any permitted-origin list in the file. Because corsMiddleware is registered globally at index.ts:33 (before authMiddleware at :34), any attacker-controlled web page can issue credentialed cross-origin reads of every API response. Missing origin-allowlist control.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The described code and structure are genuinely present and I verified each element myself. cors.ts:17-24 passes @koa/cors an origin function that returns ctx.get(\\\"Origin\\\") || \\\"*\\\" together with credentials:true, i.e. verbatim reflection of the caller's Origin with credentials enabled. I grepped the entire backend for NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL|allowedOrigin|allowlist|whitelist and found no origin comparison anywhere (the only NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL uses are a reset link, a redirect, and a Stripe return_url), confirming the missing-allowlist claim. The middleware is globally active: index.ts:33 registers corsMiddleware before authMiddleware (:34) and all routes (:40-43), and utils/middleware.ts:3-9 shows createMiddleware is a transparent passthrough. Reachability is affirmatively confirmed by the graph (reachable_from_entry=true on lines 7, 9, 17, 19), not merely assumed. TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst. (1) Location: the analyst's headline sink at lines 7-9 is DEAD CODE, guarded by `if (ctx.method === \\\"options\\\")` (lowercase) while Koa's ctx.method is the raw uppercase HTTP method \\\"OPTIONS\\\", so that branch never executes; the live vulnerable policy is solely lines 17-24, which @koa/cors applies to preflight and simple requests alike. (2) Impact: the claim of \\\"credentialed cross-origin reads of every API response\\\" is overstated because there are NO ambient credentials \u2014 zero `cookie` references exist repo-wide, and auth is Bearer JWT pulled from localStorage and attached manually (frontend/utils/fetcher.ts:15-18; authMiddleware reads only the Authorization header at auth/utils.ts:88-112). A cross-origin page cannot read the victim's localStorage and browsers never auto-attach Authorization, so credentials:true does not enable session-riding of authenticated endpoints. The defect is still real: any origin can read responses from the no-auth publicRoutes (auth/utils.ts:62-78, e.g. /v1/runs/.+/public, /v1/datasets/.+, /v1/template_versions/latest), including from an instance bound to localhost or an intranet host, using the victim's browser as a network pivot. Severity is therefore MEDIUM rather than the HIGH a cookie-session app would warrant. Dependency note: @koa/cors is ^5.0.0 (package.json:12), which is at/past the fix for the known CVE-2023-49803 advisory (affecting <5.0.0), so this is the application's own misconfiguration, not a library CVE; the exploit catalog was unavailable (\\\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\\\") so no module is cited."
