          "ruleId": "CWE-693",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "No HTTP security-response-header control exists in either package. The backend's entire Koa middleware chain was fully enumerated (index.ts lines 29-43): requestHandler, tracingMiddleWare, errorMiddleware, logger(), corsMiddleware, authMiddleware, ratelimit, bodyParser(), setDefaultBody, then four routers \u2014 there is no helmet/koa-helmet or any header-setting middleware. The frontend's nextConfig object keys were likewise fully enumerated (next.config.js: reactStrictMode, eslint, typescript, images, rewrites, webpack, transpilePackages) \u2014 there is no `headers()` function. A repo-wide search for Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options, Strict-Transport-Security, X-Content-Type-Options, helmet, csrf and SameSite returned zero rows. So the app ships with no CSP, no HSTS, no X-Frame-Options/clickjacking defence, no X-Content-Type-Options, and no CSRF token middleware.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): I independently re-derived the core claim and it holds: neither package sets any HTTP security response header. Verified from four directions I checked myself. (1) Source: packages/backend/src/index.ts:29-43 contains the complete Koa chain (requestHandler, tracingMiddleWare, errorMiddleware, logger(), corsMiddleware, authMiddleware, ratelimit, bodyParser(), setDefaultBody, then redirections/v1/auth/webhooks routers) with no helmet/koa-helmet or header-setting middleware; my own Cypher returned exactly those 13 app.use calls at lines 29-43. (2) packages/frontend/next.config.js:1-56 defines only reactStrictMode, eslint, typescript, images, rewrites, webpack, transpilePackages -- no headers() function, and Next ^14.1.0 emits no security headers by default. (3) A repo-wide case-insensitive grep for helmet|Content-Security-Policy|X-Frame-Options|Strict-Transport-Security|X-Content-Type-Options|frameguard|csrf|xsrf|SameSite returned zero matches, and my Cypher equivalent returned n=0. (4) I closed the escape hatches the analyst did not check: there is no vercel.json, nginx conf, Caddyfile, Dockerfile or compose file anywhere (so no infra-layer headers), no Next middleware.ts, pages/_document.tsx:1-16 sets no CSP meta tag, no helmet/koa-csrf in any package.json (only @koa/cors), and every backend ctx.set() call is CORS or Content-Type/Content-Disposition only. TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst. First, the CSRF-token and SameSite portions of the claim are a non-finding: authentication is a Bearer JWT read from the Authorization header (packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts:88,112) and held in localStorage (packages/frontend/utils/fetcher.ts:15,30,63,74,84), so there are no session cookies, a cross-site request carries no ambient credentials, CSRF tokens are not architecturally required, and SameSite has no auth cookie to apply to. Second, severity is LOW, not the analyst's implied HIGH: this is a pure protection-mechanism/hardening gap with no attacker-controlled data flow to a sink, and it only amplifies other defects. It does amplify genuine risk here -- a one-month JWT in localStorage with no CSP means any XSS yields full token theft, and absent X-Frame-Options the authenticated dashboard is framable -- but both are conditional on a separate bug. The all-false reachable_from_entry on the app.use calls is a graph artifact for module-level bootstrap code; the source shows these plainly execute before app.listen at index.ts:46, so I did not downgrade on it. Separately and out of scope for this verdict, packages/backend/src/utils/cors.ts:7-24 reflects any Origin back with credentials:true, which is materially more serious than the missing headers and should be filed as its own finding. The advisory exploit lookup was unavailable (corpus not indexed), which does not bear on a finding about absent code rather than a versioned CVE."
