          "ruleId": "CWE-307",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "There is no brute-force / credential-stuffing protection on auth.post(\"/login\") (auth/index.ts:139): the handler looks up the account by email and calls verifyPassword with no failed-attempt counter, no account lockout, and no per-account throttle. The only protection is the single global koa-ratelimit instance (index.ts:35), and that control is doubly weakened in utils/ratelimit.ts: (a) its `whitelist: (ctx) => { if (ctx.state.userId) return true; return false }` exempts every authenticated request from rate limiting entirely, and (b) its key is `id: (ctx) => ctx.request.ip || ctx.ip || ctx.state.userId` while index.ts:26 sets `app.proxy = true`, which makes Koa derive ctx.request.ip from the client-supplied X-Forwarded-For header \u2014 so an attacker can rotate that header to get a fresh 150-requests-per-minute bucket per forged value. Missing anti-automation control on authentication.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. The unauthenticated POST /auth/login handler (auth/index.ts:139-170, router prefix \"/auth\" at :10-12) does an email lookup then verifyPassword and returns 403, with no failed-attempt counter, no account lockout, and no per-account throttle. A repo-wide case-insensitive grep for lockout|failed_attempt|loginAttempt|attempts|throttl|rate.?limit over packages/ matched ONLY utils/ratelimit.ts and its single registration, so the one global koa-ratelimit instance (index.ts:35) is the sole anti-automation control in the monorepo. That control is bypassable: index.ts:26 sets app.proxy = true and ratelimit.ts:10 keys buckets on ctx.request.ip, which in Koa ^2.15.0 (package.json:21) resolves to ips[0] -- the LEFTMOST, fully client-supplied X-Forwarded-For entry -- with maxIpsCount/proxyIpHeader never set and no nginx/real_ip/proxy_set_header config anywhere in the tree. Because Koa takes the leftmost value, the forged entry wins even behind a proxy that merely appends the true client IP, so rotating the header grants a fresh max:150-per-60s bucket per value, i.e. effectively unlimited credential-stuffing attempts; driver:\"memory\" (ratelimit.ts:3,6-7) also scopes counters per process. Reachability is established from source (mounted at index.ts:42, served at :46, and /auth/login is an explicit public route per auth/utils.ts:63) and corroborated by the graph, which lists auth/utils.ts::program:verifyPassword as an EntryPoint with exposure \"reachable\", kind \"handler\"; the uniform reachable_from_entry=false on every CpgCall in this scan (including line-1 require() nodes) is uninformative, not counter-evidence. ONE CORRECTION: the analyst's sub-claim (a) is overstated -- the whitelist at ratelimit.ts:18-23 does exempt authenticated traffic globally (authMiddleware at index.ts:34 runs before ratelimit at :35), but it does NOT weaken the login control, because /auth/login hits the public-route early return at auth/utils.ts:90-106 and never sets ctx.state.userId. Only the spoofable-key sub-claim (b) actually defeats the login limiter, and it holds. I also move the anchor from ratelimit.ts to the authentication endpoint, since CWE-307 is about the missing restriction on the auth attempt. Exploit-corpus lookup returned \"not indexed\", so no exploit-reference signal was used."
