          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "versions.get(\"/:id\") fetches the template_version by id with no ownership predicate (`select * from template_version where id = ${ctx.params.id}`) and then performs a SEPARATE scoped lookup for the parent template (`select * from template where project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and id = ${version.templateId}`) \u2014 but it never checks whether that second query returned anything before responding. It unconditionally returns `ctx.body = { ...version, template }`, so when the version belongs to another tenant the ownership query yields undefined for `template` while the full version row (including content and test_values) is still disclosed. The ownership check is performed but its result is never enforced.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly in source. `versions.get(\"/:id\")` at packages/backend/src/api/v1/templateVersions.ts:43-55 fetches the template_version row keyed only on `ctx.params.id` (L45) with no ownership predicate, then runs a separate tenant-scoped lookup for the parent template (L51) but NEVER checks its result before responding with `ctx.body = { ...version, template }` (L54). There is no `if (!template)` guard or `ctx.throw` between L52 and L54, so the ownership check is performed and its result discarded. For a cross-tenant id the first query still returns the full row; `template` resolves to undefined and is dropped by JSON.stringify, leaving the victim's complete template_version (content, extra, test_values) disclosed.\n\nTwo facts I established make this more severe than the analyst assessed: (1) `template_version.id` is `serial` (init.sql:163) \u2014 a sequential integer, so enumeration is trivial with no UUID guessing; (2) `template_version` has no project_id column (init.sql:162-171), so tenancy is derivable ONLY via template_id -> template.project_id, which is exactly the discarded query \u2014 making it the sole isolation control.\n\nReachability: the graph reports reachable_from_entry=false/hops=-1 for ALL 139 calls in this file, including the `/latest` route that auth/utils.ts:73 lists as a public SDK endpoint \u2014 this is the documented arrow-function under-linking artifact, not real unreachability, so I discounted it and confirmed reach from source: src/index.ts:41 (app.use(v1.routes())) -> api/v1/index.ts:42 (v1.use(templateVersions.routes())) -> prefix \"/template_versions\" (L8) + \"/:id\" (L43). The frontend actively calls it (frontend/pages/prompts/[[...id]].tsx:145).\n\nAttacker prerequisite is low: the route is not in publicRoutes so a JWT is required, but `/auth/.+` is public (auth/utils.ts:63) and `/auth/signup` is open self-registration (auth/index.ts:14). An attacker registers, supplies their OWN projectId to satisfy the org check at auth/utils.ts:117-127, then enumerates victim version ids. Deviation from intent is proven by sibling templates.ts:71,79,95, which all scope `project_id = ctx.state.projectId AND id = ctx.params.id` in a single query. Not SQLi \u2014 postgres.js parameterizes these interpolations; the defect is purely authorization. Exploit-reference check was inapplicable (corpus returned \"not indexed\"; only a generic monorepo version 1.0.0 available), so it played no part in this verdict.\n\nAdjacent and arguably worse, outside this lead's scope: versions.patch(\"/:id\") at lines 57-77 updates content/extra/test_values keyed solely on ctx.params.id with no ownership check at all \u2014 a cross-tenant write primitive worth filing separately."
