          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The Next.js image optimizer has no host restriction: next.config.js sets `images.remotePatterns = [{ protocol: \"https\", hostname: \"**\" }]`, a wildcard that permits any HTTPS host. The enumerated nextConfig keys contain no `domains` allowlist or any narrower pattern. This turns /_next/image into an open server-side fetch proxy \u2014 an unauthenticated attacker can supply arbitrary ?url= values to make the Next server issue outbound HTTPS requests to hosts of their choosing and reflect the responses. Missing remote-host allowlist control.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently verified against the real source. packages/frontend/next.config.js:16-23 sets images.remotePatterns = [{protocol:\"https\", hostname:\"**\"}]; \"**\" is a micromatch wildcard matching every hostname, including private IP literals and internal DNS names. I confirmed there is no narrowing control anywhere: grep for `unoptimized|images\\.|loaderFile|output: \"export\"` across packages/frontend returned no matches (no domains allowlist, no custom loader, no unoptimized, no static export), and a glob for packages/frontend/**/middleware.{ts,js,tsx} returned no files, so nothing authenticates Next's built-in routes. package.json:8 runs `next start` (a real Node server, also required by rewrites() and pages/api/app/[id].ts), so GET /_next/image?url=... is reachable unauthenticated and causes a server-side fetch of any attacker-supplied HTTPS URL. The wildcard is load-bearing rather than dead config: components/SmartViewer/Message.tsx:137-139 renders <Image src={item.imageUrl.url}> from logged LLM trace content. I correct the analyst on impact: Next's optimizer requires the upstream response to be a valid image type, so arbitrary response REFLECTION is not available \u2014 this is a mostly blind, GET-only SSRF \u2014 and because protocol is pinned to https, HTTP-only cloud metadata (169.254.169.254, GCP/Azure) is out of reach. Still genuine because Next applies no private-IP or DNS-rebinding filtering, leaving unauthenticated internal host/port probing via differential status and timing, forged GETs to internal HTTPS endpoints, and bandwidth/CPU amplification plus image-cache pollution. Hence MEDIUM, not HIGH. Two unresolved caveats that do not change the verdict: next is \"^14.1.0\" with no lockfile in the tree and zero :Dependency rows, so I could not pin an exact version and treat any dependency-CVE angle as UNKNOWN (neither exploitable-by-CVE nor patched); and the exploit corpus was unavailable (\"not indexed\"), so no module/CVE reference could be cited. The weakness is a first-party misconfiguration, not an upstream CVE, so neither gap blocks confirmation. The reachable_from_entry=false flags on the images assignments are the expected artifact of a build-time config object (sibling rewrites() calls show true), not evidence against exploitability, since the actual sink is Next's framework-internal fetch which no CPG edge from this file would model."
