          "ruleId": "CWE-770",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The global rate limiter in `packages/backend/src/utils/ratelimit.ts` is effectively neutralized along three independent axes, each marked by hedge/stale text. (1) The configured ceiling was raised to `max: 150` while the adjacent comment still reads `// limit unlogged IPs to 50 requests per minute` \u2014 the documented limit was tripled and the comment never updated, the classic signature of a quietly relaxed control. (2) `whitelist: (ctx) => { if (ctx.state.userId) return true; return false }` exempts every authenticated user from rate limiting entirely; because index.ts registers `app.use(authMiddleware)` BEFORE `app.use(ratelimit)`, `ctx.state.userId` is already populated by the time the limiter runs, so any account holder has unlimited throughput against expensive endpoints. (3) `blacklist` is present only as commented-out boilerplate. Compounding all of it, the limiter keys on `id: (ctx) => ctx.request.ip || ctx.ip || ctx.state.userId` while index.ts sets `app.proxy = true`, which makes Koa derive `ctx.request.ip` from the client-supplied X-Forwarded-For header \u2014 so even the unauthenticated 150/min bucket is trivially evaded by rotating that header.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. The repo's ONLY rate limiter (grep across packages/ finds no other limiter and no per-route throttles) is defeated two independent ways. (A) Authenticated-user exemption: ratelimit.ts:18-23 whitelists any request with ctx.state.userId. I traced the ordering dependency rather than assuming it \u2014 index.ts:34 registers authMiddleware before index.ts:35 registers ratelimit, and authMiddleware assigns ctx.state.userId = payload.userId at api/v1/auth/utils.ts:114 BEFORE calling `await next()` at utils.ts:138. So by the time the limiter runs, userId is populated and every account holder has unlimited throughput against all endpoints. Graph agrees: the ctx.state.userId fieldAccess inside the whitelist lambda is reachable_from_entry=true (uid 68262bbfa3b2f426344e7f270c604e3813daf7b2), and app.use(authMiddleware)@34 / app.use(ratelimit)@35 confirm order. (B) Attacker-controlled limiter key: ratelimit.ts:10 keys on `ctx.request.ip || ctx.ip || ctx.state.userId` while index.ts:26 sets `app.proxy = true`. I grepped all of packages/ for maxIpsCount/proxyIpHeader \u2014 neither is ever set, so Koa's maxIpsCount stays at its default 0, meaning request.ip resolves to ips[0], the LEFTMOST and fully client-supplied X-Forwarded-For entry. The unauthenticated 150/min bucket is therefore evaded by rotating that header. Impact is concrete because /auth/.+ is a public route (auth/utils.ts:63) and /auth/login performs CPU-bound argon2 verifyPassword (auth/index.ts:139,155) with no other throttle, enabling credential stuffing and CPU exhaustion. I DOWNGRADE two of the analyst's three axes as non-substantive: `max: 150` vs. the stale `// limit unlogged IPs to 50` comment (ratelimit.ts:16, present verbatim) is a documentation inconsistency, not proof of a relaxed control, since 150/min is itself a plausible limit; and the commented-out `blacklist` (ratelimit.ts:24-26) is verbatim koa-ratelimit README boilerplate, i.e. an optional feature never enabled, not a removed control. The confirmation rests on (A) and (B) alone. Note the graph marks the module-init assignments (e.g. `_tmp_1.max = 150`) reachable_from_entry=false \u2014 that is the documented arrow-function/module-init under-linking artifact, and index.ts:35 app.use(ratelimit) proves in source that the middleware is installed on the live app, so I did not downgrade on it. exploit_search was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), but no version grounding is needed: the defect is in this repo's own options object, not in koa-ratelimit ^5.1.0 (packages/backend/package.json:24)."
