          "ruleId": "CWE-620",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "`handlePasswordReset` in packages/frontend/pages/update-password.tsx is a stub whose real implementation was removed and replaced with a hardcoded success flag: `// TODO: reset password with supertoken` followed by `const ok = true`. The handler never transmits the new password anywhere \u2014 no fetcher/API call exists in the function \u2014 yet it unconditionally shows the notification `message: \"Password updated successfully\"` and then `if (ok) { Router.push(\"/\") }`. A user who believes they have rotated a compromised password has not; the old credential remains valid indefinitely with positive confirmation to the contrary. Note the same file's validator also accepts 5-character passwords (`val.length < 5`) while the sibling reset-password.tsx enforces 6.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. packages/frontend/pages/update-password.tsx:34-52 defines `handlePasswordReset`, bound to the form at line 69 (`form.onSubmit(handlePasswordReset)`). The handler destructures `password` and then never uses it: line 37 is the comment `// TODO: reset password with supertoken`, line 38 hardcodes `const ok = true`, lines 40-45 unconditionally fire `notifications.show({title:\"Success\", message:\"Password updated successfully\"})`, and lines 47-49 do `if (ok) { Router.push(\"/\") }`. No transmission of the password exists anywhere in the function \u2014 `fetcher` is not even imported into the file (imports at lines 1-18 are only @mantine/core, @mantine/form, @tabler/icons-react, @/utils/errors, next-seo, next/router, react, @mantine/notifications). The graph agrees: of 87 CpgCall nodes with file_path='packages/frontend/pages/update-password.tsx', none is a fetch/fetcher/axios/XHR call. The secondary claim also holds: the validator at line 30 is `val.length < 5` while sibling packages/frontend/pages/reset-password.tsx:38 enforces `val.length < 6`. This is a silent failure of a security control that returns affirmative success \u2014 CWE-620 (unverified password change) / CWE-754. The route is live: Next.js pages router auto-serves /update-password and next.config.js declares no rewrite or redirect excluding it. I am downgrading the analyst's severity, however, based on scoping evidence the analyst did not establish: (1) the genuine password-reset flow is a DIFFERENT, working page \u2014 packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/index.ts:182 emails `${NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL}/reset-password?token=...`, and reset-password.tsx:51 correctly POSTs to /auth/reset-password, which hashes and persists the new credential (auth/index.ts:205-209); so no user following a reset email reaches the stub; (2) a repo-wide grep for the string `update-password` across /tmp/workspaces/9a7a7d896a81f3de/tree returns ZERO matches \u2014 the page is an unreferenced orphan route, linked from no nav, email, or settings page (packages/frontend/pages/settings.tsx contains no password field); (3) `/update-password` is missing from Layout's isAuthPage allowlist (packages/frontend/components/Layout/index.tsx:21-27), so an unauthenticated visitor is redirected to /login by lines 53-55, leaving only an already-signed-in user who manually types the URL. There is no attacker-controlled vector and no path through the application UI, so impact is limited to a self-inflicted false sense of security for a user who guesses the URL. The defect is real and should be fixed (delete the dead page or wire it to /auth/reset-password, and align the length check to 6), but it is LOW, not MEDIUM/HIGH. No versioned third-party component is implicated, so exploit-reference lookup is not applicable to this first-party logic defect."
