          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Attacker-controlled regex pattern is compiled and executed server-side: in packages/backend/src/checks/index.ts the CHECK_RUNNERS entry with id=\"regex\" destructures `const { regex, type, field } = params` (line 98) and then builds `const re = new RegExp(regex)` (line 100), running it via `re.test(lastMsg(run[field]))` (line 102) and `run[field].match(re)` (line 106). The pattern text is never length-limited, anchored, or screened for nested quantifiers, so a stored check such as params.regex = \"(a+)+$\" or \"(x*)*y\" causes catastrophic backtracking against run input text, hanging the Node event loop (single-threaded DoS). The `params` object is attacker-supplied: runChecks.ts:83 does `params = check.params` and runChecks.ts:98 calls `runner.evaluator!(run, params)`; the check objects come from HTTP bodies at `checklists.post(\"/\")` (checklists.ts:33, `const { slug, type, data } = ctx.request.body`), `checklists.patch(\"/:id\")` (checklists.ts:54), `radars.post(\"/\")` (radars.ts:217, `const { description, view, checks, ... } = ctx.request.body`) and `radars.patch(\"/:radarId\")` (radars.ts:243). The radar cron job then re-runs the stored pattern over many runs (jobs/radar.ts:12 `runChecksOnRun(run, checks, true)`), amplifying the stall.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source independently. The regex CHECK_RUNNERS entry at packages/backend/src/checks/index.ts:96-112 destructures `regex` straight out of `params` (line 98), compiles it with `new RegExp(regex)` (line 100), and executes it via `re.test(lastMsg(run[field]))` (line 102) and `run[field].match(re)` (line 106) with no length cap, no anchoring, and no nested-quantifier screening. A grep for safe-regex|re2|RE2|recheck across packages/backend/src returns zero hits, so there is no regex guard or timeout anywhere in the backend; Node's RegExp cannot be interrupted, so a pattern like \"(a+)+$\" blocks the single-threaded event loop. `params` is genuinely attacker-supplied: runChecks.ts:83 reads `const { id, params } = check` and runChecks.ts:98 invokes `runner.evaluator!(run, params)`, while the storage routes apply no schema validation at all -- checklists.ts:33-52 inserts `data` from ctx.request.body verbatim (line 13 reads \"// TODO: full zod\") and radars.ts:217-241/243-276 store `sql.json(checks)` raw, even though zod is used on other routes (auth, orgs, projects, datasets). shared/filters/index.ts:177-181 confirms `regex` is a free-text {type:\"text\"} param with only a placeholder, no pattern or maxLength. Reachability is confirmed both ways: the graph reports uid 4fd616b28cd7a95aa6546a62c8b902b8271c6497 (new RegExp, line 100) and the `test` call at line 102 as reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, and the source shows two live drivers -- jobs/radar.ts:12 inside a while(true) loop over 300-run batches (radar.ts:91-105, entrypoint src/radar.ts:3), and, uncited by the analyst but stronger, api/v1/evaluations/index.ts:153 which runs the stored checks inside the HTTP handler POST /v1/evaluations/run, a route listed in publicRoutes at auth/utils.ts:77, with the match subject (`output`) supplied in the same request body -- so an unauthenticated caller holding only a project UUID stalls the main API event loop. Correcting the analyst: their headline evidence, a FLOWS_TO self-loop on the new RegExp call allegedly \"proving its own argument is tainted\", proves no such thing (a self-loop is a graph artifact), but the claim survives on the source reading above. Severity MEDIUM rather than HIGH: planting the pattern needs a project-member credential and impact is denial-of-service only, though the public trigger route and the uninterruptible regex keep it well above LOW."
