          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Over-broad regex literal in the authentication allowlist exposes mutating dataset endpoints to unauthenticated callers. packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts:76 adds `new RegExp(`/v1/datasets/.+`)` to `publicRoutes` with the comment \"getDataSets in SDKs\" (read-only intent), but authMiddleware (line 81) evaluates it as `publicRoutes.some((route) => typeof route === \"string\" ? route === ctx.path : route.test(ctx.path))` (line 85) with NO HTTP-method discrimination. Because the datasets router is `new Router({prefix: \"/datasets\"})` (datasets/index.ts:9) mounted on `const v1 = new Router({prefix: \"/v1\"})` (api/v1/index.ts:18) via `v1.use(datasets.routes())` (api/v1/index.ts:39), the literal matches every dataset sub-route including the destructive ones: `datasets.delete(\"/:id\")` (line 111), `datasets.patch(\"/:id\")` (line 96), `datasets.delete(\"/prompts/:id\")` (line 166), `datasets.patch(\"/prompts/:id\")` (line 176), `datasets.delete(\"/variations/:id\")` (line 202), `datasets.patch(\"/variations/:variationId\")` (line 211), `datasets.post(\"/prompts\")` (line 120), `datasets.post(\"/variations\")` (line 243). authMiddleware then does `if (isPublicRoute && (!bearerToken || validateUUID(bearerToken))) { ... await next(); return }`, skipping verifyJwt entirely. The delete handler compounds this by not scoping to the project at all: `await sql`delete from dataset where id = ${id}``. Failure scenario: an unauthenticated `DELETE /v1/datasets/<uuid>` with no Authorization header matches the regex, bypasses JWT verification, and deletes any tenant's dataset (ctx.status = 200).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. auth/utils.ts:76 puts the unanchored literal `new RegExp(`/v1/datasets/.+`)` (commented \"getDataSets in SDKs\", i.e. read-only intent) into `publicRoutes`, and authMiddleware matches it on `ctx.path` ONLY (lines 84-86) with zero HTTP-method discrimination, then short-circuits at lines 90-96 (`if (isPublicRoute && (!bearerToken || validateUUID(bearerToken))) { await next(); return }`) so `verifyJwt` on line 112 is never reached. With no Authorization header, bearerToken is undefined, `!bearerToken` is true, and validateUUID(undefined) returns false (utils/misc.ts:20-21), so the request passes through unauthenticated. I verified this is the ONLY auth layer in the backend and that it is global: grep for authMiddleware across packages/backend/src yields exactly one consumer, src/index.ts:34 `app.use(authMiddleware)`, registered app-wide before `app.use(v1.routes())` (index.ts:41); I read all 266 lines of datasets/index.ts and there is no route-level guard, and no checkAccess-style helper exists anywhere. Mounting confirms coverage: datasets/index.ts:9 `new Router({prefix:\"/datasets\"})` mounted via api/v1/index.ts:39 `v1.use(datasets.routes())` under api/v1/index.ts:18 `new Router({prefix:\"/v1\"})`, so the regex matches every /v1/datasets/<x> sub-route including the mutating ones (delete \"/:id\" :111, patch \"/:id\" :96, post \"/prompts\" :120, delete \"/prompts/:id\" :166, patch \"/prompts/:id\" :176, delete \"/variations/:id\" :202, patch \"/variations/:variationId\" :211, post \"/variations\" :243, plus unscoped reads at :149 and :188). The damage is compounded because the delete handler has no tenant predicate at all (datasets/index.ts:111-117: `delete from dataset where id = ${id}`), unlike the patch at :105 which does scope on project_id; the same is true of :169-170, :205, :183, :235. Regarding the graph's reachable_from_entry=false on all dataset routes: that is a graph artifact, not counter-evidence -- I queried src/index.ts and `app.listen(PORT)` and every `app.use(...)` are also reachable_from_entry=false/hop_distance=-1, so the graph models neither top-level module execution nor Koa dispatch; the source wiring establishes reach. Advisory exploit-reference step could not be run (corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\") and no version is pinned in packages/backend/package.json, but this is a first-party authorization logic flaw, so the verdict rests on source evidence alone. Net effect: an unauthenticated `DELETE /v1/datasets/<uuid>` bypasses JWT verification and destroys any tenant's dataset (ctx.status = 200). Analyst's location and CWE are correct; I extend the end line to 96 to cover the actual bypass branch."
