          "ruleId": "CWE-625",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "All four regex literals in the `publicRoutes` authentication allowlist (packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts lines 63, 71, 72, 76) are missing `^` and `$` anchors, and are consumed with `route.test(ctx.path)` (line 85), which has SUBSTRING semantics \u2014 unlike the plain-string entries in the same array, which are compared with strict equality (`route === ctx.path`). The literals are `new RegExp(`/auth/.+`)`, `new RegExp(`/v1/runs/.+/public`)`, `new RegExp(`/v1/runs/.+/feedback`)` and `new RegExp(`/v1/datasets/.+`)`. Consequently ANY request path that merely CONTAINS one of these fragments is classified as public and skips verifyJwt \u2014 e.g. a path like `/v1/users/auth/x` or `/v1/orgs/v1/datasets/x` satisfies `isPublicRoute`. `.+` also matches `/`, so the patterns span arbitrarily many path segments rather than one. This makes the allowlist's blast radius depend on route-table coincidences instead of an explicit list, and any future route whose path embeds `/auth/` or `/v1/datasets/` silently becomes unauthenticated. Anchoring each literal (`^/v1/datasets/[^/]+$`) and splitting by HTTP method is the fix.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived. packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts:62-78 defines publicRoutes with four unanchored RegExp literals (L63 `/auth/.+`, L71 `/v1/runs/.+/public`, L72 `/v1/runs/.+/feedback`, L76 `/v1/datasets/.+`) \u2014 no `^`/`$` \u2014 consumed at L84-86 via `publicRoutes.some((route) => typeof route === \"string\" ? route === ctx.path : route.test(ctx.path))`, i.e. unanchored substring matching for the regex entries while string entries use strict equality. authMiddleware is mounted globally at src/index.ts:34 ahead of every router and is the ONLY auth layer (grep of packages/backend/src/utils found no other access-control middleware); the isPublicRoute branch at L90-106 calls next() without verifyJwt and never sets ctx.state.userId/orgId. Graph confirms exploitability: authMiddleware is an :EntryPoint {kind:\"handler\", exposure:\"reachable\"} and the `.some(...)` call at L84 has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1 (the L85 `route.test` node's reachable_from_entry=false is the known arrow-function under-linking, so no downgrade). The impact is concrete today, not merely latent: because `.` matches `/`, `/v1/datasets/.+` spans multiple segments and matches every route in api/v1/datasets/index.ts, not just the single SDK GET its comment intends \u2014 with no Authorization header at all, DELETE /v1/datasets/prompts/:id (L166-173 `delete from dataset_prompt where id = ${id}`), DELETE /v1/datasets/:id (L111-117 `delete from dataset where id = ${id}`), DELETE/PATCH /v1/datasets/variations/:id (L202-240) and GET /v1/datasets/prompts|variations/:id (L149, L188) all execute unauthenticated with no project/org filter, giving unauthenticated cross-tenant read and destructive write. The analyst's `/v1/users/auth/x` example is correct as regex behaviour but is latent only (no current route embeds `/auth/` outside the intentionally public /auth router, auth/index.ts:11). Exploit-reference check not applicable: first-party code, no pinned component version (root package.json \"1.0.0\"), and exploit_search returned \"corpus unavailable: not indexed\" \u2014 the verdict rests solely on source and graph evidence."
